{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["And the reason of <b>\"and you shall slaughter, etc., as I have commanded you\"</b>: according to the opinion of our Sages (Ḥullin 28a), \"as I have commanded you\" — orally, as a law given to Moses from Sinai — teaches that Moses was commanded concerning the trachea and the esophagus, and concerning the majority of one organ in a bird and the majority of two organs in an animal. And know that שְׁחִיטָה in the sacred tongue is the severing of the signs of the neck. And do not be troubled on account of what it says, וַיִּשְׁחָטֵם בַּמִּדְבָּר (Numbers 14:16), for it is a figure of speech, that he slaughtered them like sheep. And behold, He commanded in the offerings <b>וְשָׁחַט</b> and <b>וּשְׁחָטוֹ</b>, which is by the signs of the neck. Now at first He commanded that everything they eat be whole and slaughtered according to the law of offerings, and now, when He comes to permit the meat of unconsecrated food, He said, וְזָבַחְתָּ מִבְּקָרְךָ וּמִצֹּאנְךָ כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוִּיתִיךָ — when they were offerings — וְתֹאכַל אוֹתָם חֻלִּין בְּכָל תַּאֲוַת נַפְשֶׁךָ — meaning that He permits them as unconsecrated food in any place, provided only that they be slaughtered as He commanded at first, when they were all offerings. And this is the straightforward meaning of this verse, correctly understood. And perhaps our Sages intended this very thing when they said in the Sifrei (Re'eh 39): כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוִּיתִיךָ — just as consecrated offerings require שְׁחִיטָה, so too unconsecrated food requires שְׁחִיטָה. And behold, the commandment of שְׁחִיטָה is explicitly stated here, and elucidated from the words of our Sages in the received tradition."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:49:06.901141877Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}